Welcome to the Nginx web server kit. This kit is designed to help understand how to query and get a start at analyzing logs generated by your Nginx web servers.

This playbook serves to get you oriented around the kit and provide some anchors into the data and dashboards.

# Integration Guide
An integration guide is available on [this documentation page](https://docs.gravwell.io/integrations/application/nginx.html)

# Overview

There are 2 tags associated with this kit. The primary tag ($NGINX) contains access logs, and the error tag ($NGINX_ERR) captures errors from the web server. 

Configuration macros are used in the queries to easily swap in whichever tag you choose to use. 

- $NGINX - the integration guide assumes you use tag=nginx on ingester, and have configured Nginx for json output
- $NGINX_ERR - the integration guide assumes you use tag=nginx-err

Queries cover the whole tag. To look at one site, filter on the vhost field (see below). To look at one server, filter on the [src field](https://docs.gravwell.io/search/src/src.html).

## Nginx Connection Logs
The kit expects the JSON log format from the [integration guide](https://docs.gravwell.io/integrations/application/nginx.html), including the query and vhost fields. If your log format doesn't have them, add a "query" field set to $query_string after the "uri" line, and a "vhost" field set to $server_name as the last field.

The uri field is the path only, so without the query field these detections never fire: Nginx - Web Open Redirect Attempt, Nginx - SQL Injection Strings In URI, Nginx - Webshell Regeorg Detection Via Web Logs, Nginx - Windows Webshell Strings, and Nginx - Cross Site Scripting Strings. Ten more check both the path and the query string, and miss attacks carried in the query string.

The vhost field is the name of the server block that handled the request, so sites on the same server can be told apart. It's the first name in that block's server_name, so list each site's main hostname first. A catch-all block (server_name _;) logs _, which marks requests that matched no named site, such as scanners hitting the server's IP. The Site Health and Requests By Site Over Time queries need this field and return nothing without it, and logs written before you add it have no vhost.

```
tag=$NGINX ax
| table
```

Connection logs contain the following fields:

- time
- remote_addr
- method
- uri (the path only)
- query (the query string, without the leading ?)
- status
- bytes_sent
- referer
- user_agent
- request_time (seconds)
- upstream (the backend address; empty for content nginx serves itself)
- vhost

## Nginx Error Logs
Error logs are assumed to be in nginx's default error log format. An autoextractor is configured for tag=nginx-err, and can be accessed with the ax processor. This autoextractor must be updated if you use a different tag than nginx-err.

```
tag=$NGINX_ERR ax
```

- timestamp
- loglevel (debug, info, notice, warn, error, crit, alert, emerg)
- pid
- tid
- connection_id
- message
- client_ip
- server
- request
- upstream
- host
- referer

Fields after message are only present when nginx logs them for that event. For example, config reloads have no client, and upstream failures include upstream.

# Queries
Queries are broken out into 2 types and have appropriate labels applied for reference. 

## Stats
- Primarily used by dashboards to generate metrics and data that provides an overview of activity, though can be used for ad hoc searches in query studio. Operational queries (errors, response times, upstream health, error log activity, per-site health) carry the operations label.

## Detections
- Queries built to detect very specific behavior that is indicative of malicious activity. These can be used ad hoc, serve as a basis for your own detection engineering efforts, or enabled as scheduled searches which can be used for alerts. Every detection ships with a disabled scheduled search.

# Dashboards
4 dashboards are provided in this kit. 

## Nginx - Traffic Overview
High-level statistics of connections. Volume over time, top URI, user agent frequency, top client IPs, HTTP status code distribution, and bandwidth by client.

## Nginx - Operations
Server health. Response time and server errors over time, error log levels over time, slowest URIs, upstream (backend) health, server errors by URI, top error log messages, and a per-site (vhost) health table with request volume by site.

## Nginx - Attack Surface
This dashboard may indicate suspicious activity. Queries are built with lists, and likely need to be extended and customized for your purposes, but these serve as a solid starting point.
- Provides information about errors. 
- A list of user agents are configured for suspicious UA. 
- A list of suspicious URIs that indicate probing behavior.

## Nginx - IP Investigation
Prompts for an IP address, and will provide information about connection requests coming from it. Will surface user agents, errors, HTTP status codes, methods, and an activity timeline.

# Actionables
## Nginx - IP Lookup
A variety of actions are provided for IP addresses in Nginx connection logs. These allow you to quickly pivot to searching for specific activity associated to the requesting IP

Actions:
- IP Investigation Dashboard
- IP Activity timeline
- URI and Methods
- User Agents